## Proof

Place coordinates with \(B=(0,0)\), \(C=(14,0)\). From \(AB=13, AC=15\):
\[
x_A^2+y_A^2=169,\quad (x_A-14)^2+y_A^2=225 \implies x_A=5,\; y_A=12,
\]
so \(A=(5,12)\). The semiperimeter is \(s=21\), area \(=84\), inradius \(r=4\), circumradius \(R=\tfrac{13\cdot14\cdot15}{4\cdot84}=\tfrac{65}{8}\).

**Incenter.** \(I=\frac{14A+15B+13C}{42}=(6,4)\) (indeed \(r=4\), above \(D=(6,0)\) since \(BD=s-CA=6\)).

**Circumcenter.** On \(x=7\); solving gives \(O=(7,\tfrac{33}{8})\).

**Point \(X=OI\cap BC\).** Slope of \(OI\): \(\frac{4-33/8}{6-7}=\frac{1}{8}\). Line \(OI\): \(y-\tfrac{33}{8}=\tfrac18(x-7)\). Setting \(y=0\): \(x=7-33=-26\). So \(X=(-26,0)\).

**Point \(S=AX\cap (ABC)\), \(S\ne A\).** Parametrize line \(AX\): \(P(t)=(5,12)+t(-31,-12)\). Substituting into \((x-7)^2+(y-\tfrac{33}{8})^2=\tfrac{4225}{64}\):
\[
(2+31t)^2+\Big(\tfrac{63}{8}-12t\Big)^2=\tfrac{4225}{64}
\;\Longrightarrow\; -65t+1105t^2=0,
\]
giving \(t=0\) (point \(A\)) and \(t=\tfrac{13}{221}\). Hence
\[
S=\Big(5-\tfrac{403}{221},\,12-\tfrac{156}{221}\Big)=\Big(\tfrac{54}{17},\,\tfrac{192}{17}\Big).
\]

**Orthocenter \(H\) of \(\triangle BIC\).** With \(B=(0,0), I=(6,4), C=(14,0)\): the altitude from \(I\) to \(BC\) is \(x=6\); the altitude from \(B\) to \(IC\) (slope of \(IC\) is \(-\tfrac12\), so perpendicular slope \(2\)) is \(y=2x\). Their intersection:
\[
H=(6,12).
\]

**Arc midpoint \(N\) of arc \(BAC\).** The midpoint of the minor arc \(BC\) (not containing \(A\)) lies on \(x=7\) below: \((7,-4)\). Its antipode through \(O=(7,\tfrac{33}{8})\) is
\[
N=\Big(7,\,2\cdot\tfrac{33}{8}-(-4)\Big)=\Big(7,\tfrac{49}{4}\Big),
\]
which is the midpoint of the major arc \(BAC\).

**Collinearity.**
\[
\text{slope}(SH)=\frac{192/17-12}{54/17-6}=\frac{-12/17}{-48/17}=\frac14,\qquad
\text{slope}(HN)=\frac{49/4-12}{7-6}=\frac14.
\]
So \(S,H,N\) are collinear, confirming the hypothesis.

**Ratio.** Compute the displacement vectors:
\[
\overrightarrow{SH}=H-S=\Big(\tfrac{48}{17},\tfrac{12}{17}\Big)=\tfrac{48}{17}(1,\tfrac14),
\]
\[
\overrightarrow{HN}=N-H=(1,\tfrac14).
\]
Thus \(\overrightarrow{SH}=\tfrac{48}{17}\,\overrightarrow{HN}\) (same direction, so the order is \(S\!-\!H\!-\!N\)), and therefore
\[
\frac{SH}{HN}=\frac{48}{17}.
\]

\[
\boxed{\dfrac{SH}{HN}=\dfrac{48}{17}}
\]

